  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b5bd5e60e42c9624b876a36ed587e020, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  infoTodisplay: The Marsh Rabbit (Sylvilagus palustris) is a slightly smaller, darker
    version of the more familiar eastern cottontail rabbit, which is best known for
    its conspicuous cotton puff tail. In contrast, the marsh rabbit has a small gray-brown
    tail. It is found throughout Florida and due to being a strong swimmer prefers
    to make its home near the water. Their diet consists mainly of the various plants
    found in and around wetlands. Breeding occurs year-round but peaks December through
    June. On average, a female marsh rabbit produces six or seven litters of two
    to four young per litter each year. By four weeks of age, the young rabbits are
    weaned and are foraging for themselves. As nocturnal creatures their most active
    at dusk and night.
  TextBoxToReveal: {fileID: 7846115802583224061}
  CanvasBase: {fileID: 4793387187504746561}
  interactAudio: {fileID: 2917461513320227906}
