    m_Alignment: 4
    m_AlignByGeometry: 0
    m_RichText: 1
    m_HorizontalOverflow: 0
    m_VerticalOverflow: 0
    m_LineSpacing: 1
  m_Text: The Marsh Rabbit (Sylvilagus palustris) is a slightly smaller, darker version
    of the more familiar eastern cottontail rabbit, which is best known for its conspicuous
    cotton puff tail. In contrast, the marsh rabbit has a small gray-brown tail.
    It is found throughout Florida and due to being a strong swimmer prefers to make
    its home near the water. Their diet consists mainly of the various plants found
    in and around wetlands. Breeding occurs year-round but peaks December through
    June. On average, a female marsh rabbit produces six or seven litters of two
    to four young per litter each year. By four weeks of age, the young rabbits are
    weaned and are foraging for themselves. As nocturnal creatures their most active
    at dusk and night.
